  "answers": {
    "q1_ball_count_range": "**Answer A (ball count) dominates.** Macro F1 vs n_balls spearman=-0.494, vs split_count spearman=-0.455. Mean balls range from 19.4 (25%) to 90.8 (150%). Peak at 25% (F1=0.571) includes under-granulation artefacts (\u22643 balls on 8 runs); among 50\u2013100% budgets, peak is 100%.",
    "q2_peak_before_max": "Performance is relatively flat between 75\u2013125% budgets: best 50\u2013100% budget is 100% vs rho0.90 reference 100% (F1=0.546). 75%: F1=0.542, 100%: 0.546, 125%: 0.564. Over-refinement beyond rho0.90 (125\u2013150%) does not consistently improve F1.",
    "q3_boundary_vs_ball_count": "**Ball count explains performance better than boundary score** (spearman -0.494 vs 0.091 for log-boundary). Geometry quality at a fixed budget is secondary to complexity level: fewer balls consistently yield higher macro F1. Boundary score still carries weak positive signal but is much weaker than fragmentation.",
    "q4_stopping_criterion": "A global stopping criterion should optimize **cumulative ball-count budget** near 75\u2013100% of the rho0.90 reference (~78 balls mean), not maximum purity or per-split utility. Split count (spearman=-0.455) and ball count (-0.494) are the primary negative controls; log-boundary (0.091) is a secondary positive signal. Stop when the budget is exhausted, not when local purity stops improving."
